WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.320
A small group of cells are all equal.

2
00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:07.820
Every one could become a nerve cell.

3
00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:09.680
Why does only one?

4
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:15.200
In the fly embryo, the cells that will make the nervous system sit in small groups.

5
00:00:16.183 --> 00:00:19.203
Every cell in a group can become a neural precursor.

6
00:00:20.023 --> 00:00:21.563
Yet usually only one does.

7
00:00:22.533 --> 00:00:25.433
If the cells start equal, something must break the tie.

8
00:00:26.417 --> 00:00:28.217
The answer comes in two steps.

9
00:00:29.177 --> 00:00:32.557
First, proneural genes make a group of cells able to become neural.

10
00:00:33.617 --> 00:00:36.757
Then the cells signal to each other through Notch, and one wins.

11
00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.320
Keep three maps in mind.

12
00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:44.980
On the embryo's underside, one cell from each cluster moves inward as a neuroblast.

13
00:00:46.083 --> 00:00:49.363
A neuroblast is a neural stem cell of the fly embryo.

14
00:00:50.223 --> 00:00:53.043
The rest of the cluster becomes epidermis, the skin.

15
00:00:54.083 --> 00:00:59.023
On the notum, the back of the fly's thorax, small clusters make the large bristles.

16
00:00:59.903 --> 00:01:01.883
Each gives one sensory organ precursor

17
00:01:03.017 --> 00:01:05.537
That cell builds one bristle and its neuron.

18
00:01:06.417 --> 00:01:07.897
The rest become epidermis.

19
00:01:08.857 --> 00:01:10.557
Small bristles come from broader stripes.

20
00:01:11.567 --> 00:01:14.167
The third map is the vertebrate neural tube.

21
00:01:14.967 --> 00:01:16.547
There, the cells are already neural.

22
00:01:17.567 --> 00:01:22.167
Now step one. A proneural gene first comes on in a whole cluster.

23
00:01:23.107 --> 00:01:24.487
Think of it as a casting call.

24
00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:31.750
In 1993, Mar Ruiz-Gómez and Alain Ghysen followed the proneural gene achaete in

25
00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:32.810
staged fly embryos.

26
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.620
It came on first in clusters of a few cells.

27
00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:41.000
Later, one cell in each cluster held much more achaete RNA.

28
00:01:42.133 --> 00:01:44.373
That cell became the neural progenitor.

29
00:01:45.193 --> 00:01:48.093
They called the first state competent, and the second determined.

30
00:01:49.167 --> 00:01:52.227
Predict. Take Notch away from the embryo.

31
00:01:53.187 --> 00:01:54.327
What happens to the clusters?

32
00:02:00.867 --> 00:02:03.407
The clusters still form in their normal places.

33
00:02:04.207 --> 00:02:07.147
But high achaete is no longer limited to one cell.

34
00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:10.020
So Notch does not place the clusters.

35
00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:14.580
It narrows them. Placing and picking are separate events.

36
00:02:15.583 --> 00:02:16.983
That leaves three puzzles.

37
00:02:17.843 --> 00:02:18.863
Who may become neural?

38
00:02:19.723 --> 00:02:23.303
Who does? And does the logic carry over to vertebrates?

39
00:02:24.367 --> 00:02:25.927
Here is the idea to carry.

40
00:02:26.847 --> 00:02:30.047
In the fly, proneural factors say which cells may become neural.

41
00:02:31.087 --> 00:02:32.067
Notch says which one does.
